course: dictionary
lesson: 6847
title: Outshine: better or brighter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To outshine someone is to be much better or more impressive than them. To outshine something is also to shine more brightly than it. Context tells you which meaning the writer means. «In the school play, Maya's song outshone everyone else's.» Nobody's song is literally glowing, so this means Maya was far better.
explain: Now try the light meaning. «Venus outshines every star in the night sky.» Venus is a planet, and it looks brighter than any star. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Put "shines more brightly than" in place of outshines. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Outshine changes its form with time. Today you outshine, yesterday you outshone, and you are outshining right now. Notice the word part out at the front. It often shows one thing going beyond another.
fact: Outshine means to be much better or more impressive than someone or something.
fact: Outshine also means to shine more brightly than something else.
fact: The past tense of outshine is outshone, as in «Her idea outshone all the others.»
fact: «Venus outshines every star in the night sky» means Venus looks brighter than any star.
fact: «An explosion that for weeks can outshine a whole galaxy» means a supernova can, for a few weeks, be brighter than a whole galaxy of stars.
exercise: «In the race, the new player outshone the team captain.» What does outshone mean here? | Shone a light on | Ran away from | Lost the ball to | *Was much better than
exercise: «Venus outshines every star in the night sky.» What does outshines mean here? | Hides behind | *Shines more brightly than | Moves around the sun faster than | Is smaller than
exercise: Which word is the past tense of outshine? | outshines | outshadow | outshining | *outshone
test: «Stars more than about 8 times as heavy as the Sun die violently in a supernova, an explosion that for weeks can outshine a whole galaxy.» What does outshine mean in this sentence? | Fall apart slowly | Hide from | *Shine more brightly than | Turn into ice
test: Which sentence uses outshine to mean much better? | *«Her clever idea outshone all the others.» | The sun rose at six. | The rain fell all day. | The lamp sat on the table.
exam: Jayden says outshine means to hide. Is he right? | *No, it means to be much better or to shine more brightly | No, it means to sleep softly | Yes, it means to hide from view | Yes, it means to fall down
